From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 00:44:26 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] virtio-gpu-virgl: retire a destroyed context's ring fences

A fenced command with VIRTIO_GPU_FLAG_INFO_RING_IDX gets a context fence
from virgl_renderer_context_create_fence() and is answered only when
virglrenderer reports that fence (write_context_fence, or the async
fence bottom half); virgl_write_fence() skips such commands
(hw/display/virtio-gpu-virgl.c:1178 in qemu 11.1.2). Two cases leave one
in g->fenceq for good:

1. CTX_DESTROY while ring fences are pending.
   virgl_cmd_context_destroy() (virtio-gpu-virgl.c:506-515) only calls
   virgl_renderer_context_destroy(), which just removes the context
   (virglrenderer 1.3.0 src/virglrenderer.c:283). For a Venus (proxy)
   context, proxy_context_destroy() frees the pending fences of every
   timeline without retiring them (src/proxy/proxy_context.c:528-533).
   The guest does not wait for its fences first: the Linux driver's
   virtio_gpu_driver_postclose() sends CTX_DESTROY when the DRM file is
   closed, e.g. when a process exits or is killed, and frees the ctx_id
   for reuse.

2. virgl_renderer_context_create_fence() fails; it returns -EINVAL for
   an unknown ctx_id (src/virglrenderer.c:430-432). The error is logged
   and the command left unanswered (virtio-gpu-virgl.c:1140-1152), and
   virtio_gpu_process_cmdq() then queues it on fenceq
   (hw/display/virtio-gpu.c:1181).

Such a command is never answered: the guest's descriptor and dma_fence
leak, and virtio_gpu_fence_poll() keeps re-arming its timer while fenceq
is not empty (virtio-gpu-virgl.c:1395-1396). A new context that reuses
the ctx_id would also retire the stale entries with its own fences.

After virgl_renderer_context_destroy(), answer every command still
waiting on a ring fence of that ctx_id with OK_NODATA: the context is
gone, so there is nothing left to wait for. When the context fence
cannot be created, answer the command at once with
VIRTIO_GPU_RESP_ERR_UNSPEC: an error rather than OK, because no fence
was set up and qemu cannot tell that the work is done; and not a ctx0
fence as a fallback, because that signals when vrend's GL work is done,
not when the context's ring reaches the fence. The response carries the
fence id like any fenced response, so the guest's fence still signals
and nothing waits forever.

The async fence path (VIRGL_RENDERER_ASYNC_FENCE_CB) has the same
problem, as its bottom half also retires only reported fences; the
change covers both.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 hw/display/virtio-gpu-virgl.c | 30 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 30 insertions(+)

diff --git a/hw/display/virtio-gpu-virgl.c b/hw/display/virtio-gpu-virgl.c
index e610358..8b13bbf 100644
--- a/hw/display/virtio-gpu-virgl.c
+++ b/hw/display/virtio-gpu-virgl.c
@@ -671,11 +671,35 @@ static void virgl_cmd_context_destroy(VirtIOGPU *g,
                                       struct virtio_gpu_ctrl_command *cmd)
 {
     struct virtio_gpu_ctx_destroy cd;
+#if VIRGL_VERSION_MAJOR >= 1
+    struct virtio_gpu_ctrl_command *fcmd, *tmp;
+#endif
 
     VIRTIO_GPU_FILL_CMD(cd);
     trace_virtio_gpu_cmd_ctx_destroy(cd.hdr.ctx_id);
 
     virgl_renderer_context_destroy(cd.hdr.ctx_id);
+#if VIRGL_VERSION_MAJOR >= 1
+    /*
+     * virglrenderer frees the context's pending ring fences without reporting
+     * them, so answer the commands still waiting on one: otherwise they stay
+     * in fenceq forever, and a new context reusing ctx_id would retire them.
+     */
+    QTAILQ_FOREACH_SAFE(fcmd, &g->fenceq, next, tmp) {
+        if (!(fcmd->cmd_hdr.flags & VIRTIO_GPU_FLAG_INFO_RING_IDX) ||
+            fcmd->cmd_hdr.ctx_id != cd.hdr.ctx_id) {
+            continue;
+        }
+        trace_virtio_gpu_fence_resp(fcmd->cmd_hdr.fence_id);
+        virtio_gpu_ctrl_response_nodata(g, fcmd, VIRTIO_GPU_RESP_OK_NODATA);
+        QTAILQ_REMOVE(&g->fenceq, fcmd, next);
+        g_free(fcmd);
+        g->inflight--;
+        if (virtio_gpu_stats_enabled(g->parent_obj.conf)) {
+            trace_virtio_gpu_dec_inflight_fences(g->inflight);
+        }
+    }
+#endif
 }
 
 static void virtio_gpu_rect_update(VirtIOGPU *g, int idx, int x, int y,
@@ -1325,6 +1349,12 @@ void virtio_gpu_virgl_process_cmd(VirtIOGPU *g,
             qemu_log_mask(LOG_GUEST_ERROR,
                           "%s: virgl_renderer_context_create_fence error: %s",
                           __func__, strerror(-ret));
+            /*
+             * No fence will ever retire this command (e.g. the context does
+             * not exist): fail it now instead of leaving it in fenceq. The
+             * response still carries the fence, so the guest's fence signals.
+             */
+            virtio_gpu_ctrl_response_nodata(g, cmd, VIRTIO_GPU_RESP_ERR_UNSPEC);
         }
         return;
     }
